WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.740
You may have heard
the expression

00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:03.780
“knowledge is power.”

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:06.600
Well, today we’re going
to give you more power

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:08.460
to control your
diet and lifestyle

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:10.740
by giving you the facts.

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:12.840
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:15.900
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:19.560
Did you know that most
chemotherapy drugs

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:22.920
are approved by the FDA
without evidence of benefit

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:27.120
on survival or quality of life?
Here’s our first story.

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:31.320
Over the next few decades,
the number of new cancer cases

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:35.820
will continue to skyrocket. Are
we winning the war on cancer?

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:39.060
Sadly, in general, no,

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:43.140
this despite the introduction of
hundreds of new anticancer drugs.

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:46.500
The war on cancer has been
likened to the war on terror.

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:49.680
No matter how many drone strikes
you do, it's nearly impossible

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:53.280
to kill all the bad guys, and no
matter how precise the bombing,

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:55.920
one must always consider
the collateral damage.

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:59.640
The toxicity from cancer
therapy can be debilitating,

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:04.920
and not just health-wise.
There's also the "financial toxicity."

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:08.640
Patented anticancer drugs
are priced at up to

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:10.740
nearly a thousand dollars a day.

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:14.040
Even with health insurance,
the average cost to patients

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:18.480
for stage IV breast cancer,
for example, can run $190,000.

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:23.580
It's bad enough to be fighting
for your life without bankrupting

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:27.960
your family at the same time –
a problem still common to this day.

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:34.260
Who can forget the apocryphal story
of Walter White, working two jobs

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:38.340
with health insurance and still
could not afford the cancer care?

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:42.180
Now, not everyone is willing
to start their own meth lab,

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:45.180
but many are willing
to go for broke.

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:48.000
A large proportion of cancer
patients reported their willingness

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:51.960
to declare bankruptcy or sell
their homes to pay for treatment.

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:56.220
I mean, look, aren't the high prices
justified if new and innovative

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:58.980
treatments offer significant
benefits to patients?

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:03.120
But you may be shocked
to find out that many

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:07.800
FDA-approved cancer drugs
may lack clinical benefit.

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:12.660
Well, then how did they
become FDA-approved?

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:16.440
Most approvals of cancer
drugs are based on flimsy

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:18.900
or untested surrogate endpoints,

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:23.040
and postmarketing studies rarely
validate the efficacy and safety

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:25.740
of these drugs on patient
centered endpoints.

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:27.060
Let me explain what that means.

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:30.720
New chemo drugs are increasingly
approved just based on

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:34.440
so-called surrogate endpoints, which
means instead of looking at what

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:39.240
we really care about — survival
or quality of life — they approve

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:42.780
drugs based on things like
response rate, tumor shrinkage.

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:47.160
But who cares if a tumor shrinks
if it doesn't actually extend

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:49.260
your quantity or quality of life?

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:53.940
It's kind of counterintuitive,
but just seeing a tumor shrink

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:57.840
on a CT scan or MRI
is not necessarily correlated

00:02:57.840 --> 00:02:59.940
with improvements in
survival or symptoms.

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:03.120
In fact, most studies that have
actually followed people out

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:05.280
found low correlations with survival.

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:09.840
The most recent comprehensive
analysis found 90% of studies

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:14.520
of such validation trials found little
correlation with overall survival.

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:19.260
Of 36 new chemo drugs approved
by the FDA based on these kind

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:21.540
of surrogate endpoints,
once they were actually

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:23.940
put to the test
in the real world,

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:29.280
only 1 in 7 was actually
shown to extend life,

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:32.100
and half explicitly flopped,

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:37.200
and the rest remain untested,
revealing that most cancer drug

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:41.340
approvals have not been
shown to, or do not, improve

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:43.140
clinically relevant endpoints.

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:48.240
Exorbitant drug prices are bad
enough for treatments that work,

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:52.740
but charging vulnerable patients
for drugs without evidence that

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:55.560
they actually improve patients
survival and quality of life

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:57.540
is unconscionable.

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:03.060
Why doesn't the FDA require
proof that chemo drugs

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:05.340
actually benefit patients
before approving them?

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:09.660
Drug companies say that requiring
randomized, controlled trials

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:12.480
with meaningful measures
would take too long,

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:16.620
but the study time reduction
using surrogate endpoints

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:20.340
rather than overall survival is
estimated at just 11 months.

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:24.180
So instead of it taking 7.3 years
to come to market on average,

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:26.280
it would take 8.2 years.

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:29.520
Yes, look, we want to get these
drugs out as soon as possible,

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:33.000
but only if they're actually
going to help people.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:35.640
Do cancer drugs improve
survival or quality of life?

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:39.780
You don't need to know, according
to our broken regulatory system.

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:42.900
And things aren't much
better over in Europe.

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:45.480
A systematic evaluation of
chemo drug approvals showed

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:48.060
that most entered the market
without evidence of benefit

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:49.620
on survival or quality of life.

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:52.860
And even years later, there was
still no conclusive evidence

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:55.320
that these drugs offered any
benefit, and when they did,

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:57.000
the gains were often marginal.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:59.400
That's why you see editorials
in the Journal of the

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:02.580
National Cancer Institute
referencing Hans Christian Andersen,

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:05.640
the author of the tale of
"The Emperor's New Clothes."

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:09.540
The studies all converge
on a singular conclusion:

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:14.160
only a minority of new cancer drugs
approved by US and European

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:19.200
regulatory authorities in recent years
deliver clinically meaningful benefits

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:23.580
to patients. In fact, some cancer-
related deaths may be hastened,

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:26.820
or even caused, by the toxic
effects of chemotherapy

00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:28.680
rather than the cancer itself.

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:33.600
Based on a review of tens of thousands
of cancer patients, in as many as 27%

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:37.920
of cases the cancer treatment itself
caused or hastened death.

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:41.460
Okay, but it might be worth
that risk if the potential benefit

00:05:41.460 --> 00:05:45.360
is large enough. And that's
the subject of my next video,

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:49.337
"How Much Does Chemotherapy
Improve Survival?"

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:54.300
Though we often hear new cancer
drugs described as game-changing

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:58.200
breakthroughs, most afford
much more modest benefits.

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:01.680
In my last video, I quoted
a recent editorial in the

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:05.280
Journal of the National Cancer
Institute suggesting that the majority

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:09.840
of new cancer drugs don't deliver
clinically meaningful benefits at all.

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:14.460
At least when they are
later proven to be ineffective,

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:16.140
they're pulled from
the market, right?

00:06:16.140 --> 00:06:20.700
No! Even when postmarket studies
show the new drugs to have

00:06:20.700 --> 00:06:24.600
no clinically meaningful benefit
compared to not just older drugs

00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:27.960
but compared to nothing,
compared to a sugar pill,

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:32.340
most chemo drugs retain FDA
approval and remain on the market,

00:06:32.340 --> 00:06:37.320
even at the same ridiculous prices.
In fact, the most expensive drug

00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:42.120
they looked at, the one
costing $169,836 a year,

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:46.860
did not improve overall survival at all,
and actually worsened quality of life.

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:51.540
$169,000 just to make you
feel worse with no benefit.

00:06:52.380 --> 00:06:56.820
Why pay a penny for a treatment
that doesn't actually help?

00:06:56.820 --> 00:06:59.100
And even when they
do improve survival,

00:06:59.100 --> 00:07:01.020
what does that actually mean?

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:07.140
Currently, the trend is for Big Pharma
to design large trials that may detect

00:07:07.140 --> 00:07:10.500
statistically significant,
but often trivial,

00:07:10.500 --> 00:07:12.600
differences in survival endpoints.

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:15.000
For example, check out
this famous trial.

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:20.040
Adding this second drug, erlotinib,
to gemcitabine for advanced

00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:23.940
pancreatic cancer significantly
prolonged overall survival.

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:28.140
Yeah, they suffered more side-
effects, but we're not just talking

00:07:28.140 --> 00:07:31.920
about tumor shrinkage.
They lived significantly longer.

00:07:31.920 --> 00:07:36.480
The placebo group only
lived 5.91 months, whereas

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:42.720
the added drug group survived
all the way to 6.24 months?

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:46.980
Wait a second. They only lived
a third of a month longer?

00:07:46.980 --> 00:07:49.500
That's just 10 days.

00:07:49.500 --> 00:07:53.700
All the side-effects and expense
for an average of just 10 days?

00:07:54.300 --> 00:07:59.220
That's why doctors shouldn't
use the statistical jargon ---

00:07:59.220 --> 00:08:00.930
significant improvement in survival ---

00:08:00.930 --> 00:08:03.540
while informing patients about
benefits of new treatment.

00:08:03.540 --> 00:08:06.600
When patients hear
the word "survival,"

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:11.100
they're not thinking about
a week and a half. If you put

00:08:11.100 --> 00:08:14.160
all the new chemo drugs together
approved over the last dozen years,

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:19.140
the average overall survival
benefit is 2.1 months.

00:08:19.140 --> 00:08:24.240
Now look, two months is two months,
I don't want to downplay that, but

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:28.980
time and again, surveys have indicated
that patients expect much more.

00:08:28.980 --> 00:08:32.520
Incredibly, about three-quarters
of patients with metastatic lung

00:08:32.520 --> 00:08:35.880
or colorectal cancer did
not report understanding

00:08:35.880 --> 00:08:39.660
that their chemo was not at all
likely to cure their cancer.

00:08:39.660 --> 00:08:43.860
I mean, that's the primary treatment,
but the chemo's not curative;

00:08:43.860 --> 00:08:47.400
it's just eking out a few
extra weeks or months.

00:08:47.400 --> 00:08:51.060
Why weren't the majority
of patients told that?

00:08:51.060 --> 00:08:54.180
It's not that they were
being over-optimistic,

00:08:54.180 --> 00:08:57.780
explained the researcher. They
were under the mistaken belief

00:08:57.780 --> 00:09:01.740
that the treatment offered a chance
of cure when it in fact didn't.

00:09:01.740 --> 00:09:04.560
That deprives patients
of the opportunity

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:07.980
to weigh the risks and benefits
and make their own decisions

00:09:07.980 --> 00:09:09.480
about their own body.

00:09:09.480 --> 00:09:14.280
If you ask cancer patients,
most want at least half a year

00:09:14.280 --> 00:09:17.100
to stomach the side-effects,
which suggests that most

00:09:17.100 --> 00:09:19.800
cancer patients might not
choose chemotherapy

00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:22.560
if they knew how little
they'd actually benefit.

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:26.400
But look, everyone's different.
One patient they interviewed

00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:29.640
said living even one week
longer would be worth it;

00:09:29.640 --> 00:09:33.240
whereas another said they
wouldn't even want to do chemo

00:09:33.240 --> 00:09:36.300
for two extra years of life;
they wouldn't want

00:09:36.300 --> 00:09:39.900
anything to interfere with the
quality of the time they had left.

00:09:40.500 --> 00:09:44.640
Either way, people deserve
to know the truth.

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:48.480
I find it telling that oncologists
and cancer nurses themselves

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:52.140
express less willingness to
accept intensive chemotherapy,

00:09:52.140 --> 00:09:54.360
given the associated toxicities.

00:09:55.260 --> 00:09:59.400
Most chemo drugs are cytotoxic,
meaning they work by killing off

00:09:59.400 --> 00:10:02.220
cancer cells, but they also
kill off some healthy cells

00:10:02.220 --> 00:10:05.940
as collateral damage, which
is why they can damage

00:10:05.940 --> 00:10:09.000
our nerves, cause
irreversible heart failure,

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:12.720
slough off the linings of our gut,
or damage your immune system.

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:17.340
Drug companies frequently downplay
the risks, though, for example,

00:10:17.340 --> 00:10:21.780
describing this breast cancer drug
as having acceptable side-effect

00:10:21.780 --> 00:10:26.160
profiles for most patients, or this
pancreatic cancer drug as having

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:29.940
a manageable and mostly
reversible safety profile.

00:10:29.940 --> 00:10:33.720
These were studies published
in top medical journals.

00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:37.740
Naturally, readers would take
these statements to be true.

00:10:37.740 --> 00:10:40.200
However, if you actually
look at the data,

00:10:40.200 --> 00:10:43.620
the number of serious, even life-
threatening side effects was double,

00:10:43.620 --> 00:10:47.400
or even five times higher on
the new breast cancer drug.

00:10:47.400 --> 00:10:50.280
And the manageable and
mostly reversible side-effects

00:10:50.280 --> 00:10:53.880
evidently weren't referring to those
who were killed by the drug.

00:10:54.660 --> 00:10:57.900
I like how they even included
like a cheat sheet.

00:10:57.900 --> 00:11:01.380
Acceptable toxicity.
Acceptable to whom?

00:11:01.380 --> 00:11:05.160
Manageable? Serious events
and deaths can never

00:11:05.160 --> 00:11:06.540
be considered manageable.

00:11:06.540 --> 00:11:10.200
And feasible? Who would sign
up for a drug whose toxicity

00:11:10.200 --> 00:11:12.120
could only be described
as feasible?

00:11:12.120 --> 00:11:14.880
Favorable?
Compared to what?

00:11:14.880 --> 00:11:18.300
Tolerable? That's for
the patient to decide.

00:11:18.300 --> 00:11:22.800
And any drug that kills people
can hardly be considered safe.

00:11:23.700 --> 00:11:28.440
Still, patients may very well
consider it worth the risk.

00:11:28.440 --> 00:11:32.340
For some cancers, we've
made tremendous strides.

00:11:32.340 --> 00:11:33.720
Testicular cancer, for example.

00:11:33.720 --> 00:11:36.720
There is greater than a one in
three chance that chemotherapy

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:40.200
could enable you to survive
at least to the five-year mark.

00:11:40.200 --> 00:11:44.340
The same with Hodgkin's disease,
a relatively rare form of lymphoma.

00:11:44.340 --> 00:11:48.120
But even when researchers
tried to err on the side

00:11:48.120 --> 00:11:52.350
of over-estimating the benefit,
for most common cancers —

00:11:52.350 --> 00:11:54.990
colon, lung, breast, and prostate —

00:11:54.990 --> 00:11:59.404
the chances appear to me
more like 1 or 2 percent.

00:11:59.580 --> 00:12:02.820
We would love it if you could
share with us your stories

00:12:02.820 --> 00:12:05.580
about reinventing your health
through evidence-based nutrition.

00:12:05.580 --> 00:12:08.640
Go to nutritionfacts.org/testimonials.

00:12:08.640 --> 00:12:11.940
We may share it on our social
media to help inspire others.

